ar again  journalist amin tant joins me now from  yangon  a police have stepped up raids overnight  have we seen much violence there today  uh yes although we haven't seen quite as  much violence as we saw earlier in the  week and on last sunday  there has been quite a lot more violence  than we saw in the last few days  during the night rates quite a number of  people reported seeing their neighbors  being taken  who were then subsequently beaten and in  fact we saw that one  local national league for democracy ward  official  was actually killed due to his injuries  sustained while they were being raided  and then uh regarding protests that  happened throughout the day we also saw  that police were stepping up  some of their crown dispersal methods  from the last few days which was largely  through stun grenades as well as  uh smoke tear gas today we saw that  police began to use rubber bullets as  well as live bullets again  right now the chinese government we're  hearing is saying that they're willing  to engage with all sides and all parties  to ease tensions  in myanmar what's their role in this  so the chinese government has a very  long history in myanmar  being myanmar's neighbor in terms of  sharing a land border as well as being  one of the largest economies  in the region the chinese government has  also had  a hand in the development of the country  through foreign direct investment as  well as  in the ongoing conflicts especially in  the border regions with ethnic  minorities  at times arming ethnic armed  organizations as well as  arming and supplying and supporting the  myanmar military  and so it's it's a very complicated sort  of relationship  and many in the pro-democratic camp are  definitely very suspicious of chinese  involvement  all right amen journalist in yangon  thanks very much for the update  thank you for having me
 

